course: medicine
lesson: 20
title: At the pharmacy
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A pharmacist is an expert in medicines who has studied for years: in the UK it takes a four-year university degree and a year of training on the job.
explain: When a doctor writes a prescription, an order for one medicine at one dose, the pharmacist checks the dose, your age, your allergies and your other medicines before handing it over.
explain: For small problems, like a mild rash or a sore throat, your grown-up can often go straight to the pharmacy instead of waiting for a doctor, and the pharmacist sends you on if it looks serious.
explain: Think of the pharmacist as the last safety net: the doctor chose the medicine, and the pharmacist catches the mistakes before they reach your mouth.
fact: A prescription is a doctor's order for a particular medicine at a particular dose, and the pharmacist prepares it and checks it is right.
fact: In the UK a pharmacist trains for about five years: a four-year degree and a year of training in a pharmacy.
fact: Pharmacists can give advice and medicines for many minor illnesses, and they send people to a doctor if something seems more serious.
fact: In the UK medicines are sorted into three groups: those sold in any shop, those sold only in a pharmacy, and those given only with a prescription.
fact: Pharmacy-only medicines are kept behind the counter because the pharmacist needs to ask a few questions first, not because they are poison.
fact: In England prescriptions are free for children under 16, and in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland they are free for everyone.
fact: Some medicines are not licensed (approved) for younger children, for example most cough and cold medicines are not for children under 6 in the UK, so pharmacists check the age on the packet.
fact: Pharmacists can also give some vaccines, such as the flu jab for adults, and check blood pressure.
fact: Herbal and homeopathic remedies do not have to pass the same tests as medicines, and homeopathic remedies are diluted (watered down) so much that essentially nothing of the starting substance is left.
fact: Natural does not mean safe: some herbal remedies can harm, or interact with other medicines.
fact: Pharmacists ask about past reactions to medicines so they never hand over one that has made you ill before.
fact: Old or unwanted medicines should go back to the pharmacy, not in the bin or down the toilet, where they can harm people, animals or rivers.
exercise: What is a prescription? | A doctor's order for a particular medicine at a particular dose | A receipt from the chemist's till | A list of foods you mustn't eat | A note saying you can miss school
exercise: What does the pharmacist check before handing over a prescription? | The dose, allergies and other medicines | Only that the price has been paid at the till | Only the colour and the shape of the box | Nothing at all, because the doctor already checked it
exercise: How long does a pharmacist train in the UK? | About five years | About five weeks | About six months | About one year
exercise: You have a mild rash. Where can your grown-up often go first? | The pharmacy, for advice and a cream | A&E, straight away | The dentist | Nowhere, because rashes never really matter
exercise: Why are some medicines kept behind the counter? | The pharmacist needs to ask questions first | They are poison and nearly banned by the government | They are too expensive to touch | Children might steal the boxes
exercise: What are the three UK groups of medicines? | Any-shop, pharmacy-only and prescription-only | Cheap, dear and free | Tablets, liquids and creams | Children's medicines, adults' medicines and pets'
exercise: Who pays for prescriptions in England? | Most adults pay, but under-16s don't | Everyone pays, children too | Nobody, ever, in any part of the whole UK | Only children pay
exercise: Where in the UK are prescriptions free for everyone? | Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland | Only in England, the biggest of the four nations | Only in London, because it is the capital | Nowhere in the UK; everyone has to pay
exercise: Why might a pharmacist say you're too young for a cough medicine? | It isn't licensed or safe for that age | Children's coughs are not real | The taste is much too strong for children | The pharmacist doesn't like coughs
exercise: Besides medicines, what else can pharmacists give? | Some vaccines and health checks | Small operations under anaesthetic | X-rays | Stitches for deep cuts
exercise: What is true about homeopathic remedies? | They are so diluted almost nothing of the substance is left | They are stronger than normal medicines | They are tested more than medicines | They are only made by doctors
exercise: Why does the pharmacist ask about reactions to medicines? | To avoid giving one that made you ill before | Out of nosiness | To fill in time | To decide how much the medicine should cost you
exercise: What should you do with old medicines? | Take them back to the pharmacy | Put them in the kitchen bin with the rubbish | Flush them down the toilet with plenty of water | Keep them in a drawer for next year
exercise: What does natural mean on a remedy? | Only where it came from, not that it is safe or works | That it is always safe | That it is checked more than medicines | That it can't interact with anything
exercise: Who prepares the medicine on a prescription? | The pharmacist | The receptionist at the GP | The doctor, at home | The person themselves
test: "its just a shop, the guy behind the counter is a cashier" — fair? | No, the pharmacist is a trained medicines expert | Yes, anyone can do the job | Yes, but these cashiers do train for a week or two | No, he's a doctor in disguise
test: Ollie, 4, has a cough and Dad grabs an adult cough syrup. What would the pharmacist say? | Most cough medicines aren't for under-6s, ask first | Fine, just give him a big spoon, it can't do any harm | Fine if it's honey flavour | Give him half the bottle
test: Mum asks for a medicine and the pharmacist asks "who's it for, any other meds?" Why? | To make sure it's right and safe for that person | To slow down the queue | To guess Mum's age | Because the law says pharmacists have to be nosy
test: Aunt says herbal pills can't hurt cos they're natural. Right? | No, natural remedies can harm or interact with medicines | Yes, plants are always safe | Yes, if they're from a health shop | No, but only if you eat them with milk
test: A Scottish kid and his dad both get prescriptions. Who pays? | Neither of them | Only the dad, as an adult | Only the kid, at a child's price | Both of them pay half each
test: what happens if you flush old pills down the loo? | Medicines can get into rivers and harm wildlife | They melt and vanish safely | The water company sells them | Nothing, the water at the works cleans them all away
test: Which problem should go to a doctor, not just the pharmacist? | A rash with high fever that doesn't fade under a glass | A small graze | Mild hay fever | A bit of dry skin
test: In a game the potion shop sells "Cure-All" for every illness. What's the real-life truth? | No single medicine cures everything; each works on certain problems | Real pharmacies sell Cure-All too | Natural potions cure all | Expensive medicines cure everything
test: "Why's that one locked behind the counter, is it drugs?" Best answer? | It's pharmacy-only, so the pharmacist asks a few questions first | It's illegal stuff | It's sweets for staff | It's too strong for anyone
test: What would happen if pharmacists didn't check prescriptions? | Mistakes in doses or mixes could reach patients | Medicines would work faster | Doctors would never make any mistakes, so it wouldn't matter | Nothing at all
test: You once came out in a rash from a medicine. What do you tell the pharmacist? | Its name, so they never give it again | Nothing, it's private | Only if it was an expensive one, not a cheap one | That you hate all medicine
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the provision of prescriptions to persons under the age of sixteen in England. | Such prescriptions are dispensed free of charge | Such prescriptions are charged at the adult rate | Such prescriptions are charged at half the adult rate | Such prescriptions are not dispensed at all
exam: Identify the principal reason for which certain medicaments are retained behind the pharmacy counter. | Their supply requires prior consultation with the pharmacist | Their possession by the public is prohibited by statute in all cases | Their price precludes open display | Their efficacy deteriorates in daylight
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises homeopathic preparations? | They are diluted to such a degree that essentially none of the original substance remains | They are subjected to more rigorous trials than conventional medicaments | They are invariably more potent than conventional medicaments | They may be supplied only upon prescription
exam: Determine the appropriate means of disposal of unused or expired medicaments. | Return to a pharmacy for safe disposal | Deposition in the ordinary household refuse | Discharge into the public sewerage system | Retention at home for future administration
